<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">EvoEmo: Towards Evolved Emotional Policies for LLM Agents in Multi-Turn Negotiation</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yunbo Long
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author.</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Cambridge, UK
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Liming Xu  Lukas Beckenbauer  Yuhan Liu  Alexandra Brintrup
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{yl892,lx249,ab702}@cam.ac.uk</span></a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Cambridge, UK
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Technical University of Munich, Germany
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Toronto, Canada
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The Alan Turing Institute, UK
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">l.beckenbauer@tum.de</span></a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">yuhanlydia.liu@mail.utoronto.ca</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent research on Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning in Large Language Models (LLMs) has demonstrated that agents can engage in <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">complex</span>, <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multi-turn</span> negotiations, opening new avenues for agentic AI.
However, existing LLM agents largely overlook the functional role of emotions in such negotiations, instead generating passive, preference-driven emotional responses that make them vulnerable to manipulation and strategic exploitation by adversarial counterparts.
To address this gap, we present EvoEmo, an evolutionary reinforcement learning framework that optimizes dynamic emotional expression in negotiations.
EvoEmo models emotional state transitions as a Markov Decision Process and employs population-based genetic optimization to evolve high-reward emotion policies across diverse negotiation scenarios.
We further propose an evaluation framework with two baselines—vanilla strategies and fixed-emotion strategies—for benchmarking emotion-aware negotiation.
Extensive experiments and ablation studies show that EvoEmo consistently outperforms both baselines, achieving higher success rates, higher efficiency, and increased buyer savings.
This findings highlight the importance of adaptive emotional expression in enabling more effective LLM agents for multi-turn negotiation.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.2" class="ltx_logical-block">
      
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Emotions aren’t the obstacles to a successful negotiation while they are the means.”
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">— Chris Voss, <span id="S1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Never Split the Difference</span></p>
</div>
    
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Extensive behavioral research has shown that human decision-making systematically deviates from classical economic assumptions of pure rationality, being dynamically shaped by psychological biases and emotional states rather than solely by stable personality traits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hilbert, 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baumeister et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Riaz et al., 2012</a>)</cite>.
While modern Large Language Models (LLMs) have made progress in replicating personality-driven behavioral patterns through Chain of Thought (CoT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, the role of emotion in shaping decisions remains relatively <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">understudied</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu and Long, 2025</a>)</cite>, especially compared to <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">static</span> human trait-based approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang and Hadfi, 2024</a>)</cite>.
Yet, emotional dynamics are crucial in fine-grained negotiation scenarios such as price bargaining <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, where emotions directly influence on tactical choices (e.g., frustration prompting premature concessions, or excitement triggering overly aggressive bids), with immediate consequences for negotiation outcomes.
In contrast, personality traits capture broad behavioral tendencies (e.g., a neurotic individual’s overall risk aversion) but cannot account for adaptive, moment-to-moment adjustments that define real-world negotiations.
While modern alignment techniques like Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kasbouya and Sael, 2025</a>)</cite> have enabled LLMs to recognize and respond to human emotional cues (e.g., expressing sympathy in response to distress), these capabilities remain fundamentally <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reactive</span>.
Current approaches excel at inferring emotions through CoT reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, yet they fail to leverage these insights for <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">strategic</span> purposes—namely, actively expressing emotions to influence negotiation trajectories and assert dominance across multi-turn interactions.
This limitation is particularly problematic in negotiation domains shaped by affective dynamics, most notably in price bargaining, where current LLM agents exhibit three fundamental deficiencies relative to human negotiators:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tactical Inflexibility</span>.
Human negotiators dynamically modulate emotional signals (e.g., expressing strategic anger to anchor prices or feigned patience to elicit concessions), whereas LLM agents typically default to static response patterns, rendering their behavior both predictable and susceptible to exploitation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kroczek et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adversarial Naivety.</span>
Their proficiency in emotion recognition paradoxically becomes a vulnerability: while able to detect signals such as frustration or empathy, LLM agents remain incapable of distinguishing genuine affect from manipulative tactics (e.g., feigned urgency in price negotiations) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang and Hadfi, 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Strategic Myopia.</span>
Unlike humans who proactively shape emotional trajectories across interactions (e.g., building rapport before demanding concessions) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bosse et al., 2013</a>)</cite>, LLMs lack meta-reasoning about emotional cause-and-effect, remaining reactive rather than generative in managing affective dynamics.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These deficiencies explain why LLMs, despite advanced reasoning capabilities, may systematically underperform humans in emotion-sensitive negotiations, particularly in price bargaining, where strategic emotion regulation is paramount.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, we propose <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvoEmo</span>, an evolutionary reinforcement learning framework for optimizing emotion policies for multi-turn, emotion-sensitive negotiations.
Our approach employs population-level evolutionary learning to discover optimal emotion transition rules, iteratively refining policies based on rewards achieved during negotiations.
Evolutionary operations—including crossover and mutation—enable efficient exploration of the policy space and propagation of high-reward emotional strategies.
By combining the exploration advantages of population-based optimization with the sequential decision-making framework of reinforcement learning, EvoEmo provides an effective approach for evolving complex emotional policies that that are challenging to optimize with gradient-based methods alone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, we introduce an cross-model negotiation framework comprising two baselines—vanilla and fixed-emotion strategies.
The vanilla baseline is emotion-free, with no explicit prompting regarding emotions, while the fixed-emotion baseline maintains a consistent emotional profile throughout the negotiation.
This framework enables systematic, emotion-aware benchmarking of negotiation capabilities across multiple state-of-the-art LLMs.
Through extensive experiments, we examine how different LLMs vary in their ability to strategically modulate emotional expression to influence pricing outcomes while simultaneously defending against adversarial emotional manipulation in multi-turn bargaining scenarios.
Our results show that evolutionarily optimized emotion policies substantially enhance LLM agents’ adaptive bargaining capabilities, achieving much better performance in terms of negotiation success rate, buyers’ saving rates, and efficiency (in total rounds of negotiation).
This framework establishes a new benchmark for emotion-aware negotiation research and highlights the critical importance of adaptive emotional intelligence in next-generation interactive AI systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While personality traits shape general negotiation tendencies (e.g., agreeableness correlating with cooperative strategies) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang and Hadfi, 2024</a>)</cite>, emotions dominate real-time bargaining dynamics through three mechanisms:
(1) <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Temporal Alignment</span> – negotiations unfold through rapid conversational turns (seconds to minutes), aligning in timescale more closely with emotional fluctuations (millisecond facial expressions, mood shifts over minutes), at times overriding the long-term influence cycles of personality and conditioning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Griessmair et al., 2015</a>)</cite>;
(2) <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Strategic Flexibility</span> – to maximize chance of success in negotiations humans dynamically adopt context-specific emotional states (e.g., tactical anger to signal firmness) to simulate tactical scarcity and temporarily override personality defaults (cite); and
(3) <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interactive Amplification</span> – emotional contagion is tactically leveraged to create affective feedback loops, where emotional stance shapes the response of the opponent throughoutthe exchange (e.g. feigned irritation is sparking opponent anxiety, affecting behavior over turns) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Olekalns and Druckman, 2014</a>)</cite>.
This fine-grained affective interplay drives dynamic shifts in bargaining power during human negotiations.
Current LLM-based negotiation systems fall short in replicating these dynamics:
Frameworks like AgreeMate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chatterjee et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and ACE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shea et al., 2024</a>)</cite> demonstrate proficiency in inferring emotions through CoT reasoning, but they lack mechanisms for strategic emotional adaptation—the ability to dynamically escalate or soften emotions to control negotiation dominance and maximize positive negotiation outcome on side of the represented party.
For example, AgreeMate’s modular architecture optimizes fixed buyer/seller roles but cannot evolve emotional+ strategies amid negotiation.
Similarly, ACE’s coaching system detects errors but cannot simulate the real-time emotional volatility that characterize human bargaining.
As a result, current LLM agents are largely reactive, rather than proactive, in their negotiation behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in reinforcement learning have significantly improved negotiation capabilities for LLM-based agents.
Methods such as Q-learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Watkins and Dayan, 1992</a>)</cite> and PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite> have been adapted to optimize negotiation policies via reward maximization.
More recent work such as GENTEEL-NEGOTIATOR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Priya et al., 2025</a>)</cite> further advances polite negotiation dialogue systems through mixture-of-expert reinforcement learning and specialized rewards for negotiation strategy, including politeness, and dialogue coherence.
Similarly, multi-agent evolutionary algorithms such as EvoAgent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> offer a promising pathway to enhance negotiation strategies through population-based optimization, where selection, crossover, and mutation drive emotion policy evolution.
However, these approaches face fundamental limitations for <span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">emotion-aware</span> negotiation scenarios: Generally grounded in reinforcement learning, they struggle to optimize agent policies within the sparse-reward, high-volatility nature of negotiation settings. Consequently, they remain ill-suited for the more fine-grained adaptation strategies that are required to dynamically react to opponent input and proactively maximize negotiation success outcomes as turns progress.
Further, evolutionary algorithms, though effective for global optimization, typcially evaluate candidate policies based on terminal outcomes. Subsequently, they tend to provide weak credit assignment to the nuanced interplay between consecutive emotional states that characterize human-like multi-turn negotiation behavior.
To address these limitations, our approach combines evolutionary exploration with reinforcement learning principles, enabling efficient emotion policy optimization while preserving the temporal dependencies essential for effective emotion-aware, multi-turn negotiation strategies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>EvoEmo Framework</h2>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.04310v1/workflow.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/202;" width="476" height="202" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of the workflow of the EvoEmo framework.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EvoEmo</span>, a framework for evolutionary optimization of emotional negotiation policies, as illustrated in <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 EvoEmo Framework ‣ EvoEmo: Towards Evolved Emotional Policies for LLM Agents in Multi-Turn Negotiation" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 1</span></a>. Our approach consists of two main stages:
(1) <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Negotiation Setup</span>: We configure both buyer and seller agents using state-of-the-art LLMs. Each product is tokenized with its name, description, and role-specific parameters. Sellers receive the product’s target price as the initial selling price and a cost price that establishes their minimum acceptable threshold. Both sellers and buyers are configured with complementary market information. Detailed prompt structures are provided in <a href="#S8.SS3" title="8.3 Prompts ‣ 8 Appendix ‣ EvoEmo: Towards Evolved Emotional Policies for LLM Agents in Multi-Turn Negotiation" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 8.3</span></a> in the Appendix.
(2) <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvoEmo Optimization</span>: We propose an evolved reinforcement learning framework for multi-turn negotiation simulation, in which buyers’ emotion policies <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\omega}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\omega}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are updated through an evolutionary algorithm with multi-agent dialogue simulation. Each generation begins by initializing a population of <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> emotional sequences with randomized transition patterns. These policies are evaluated through multi-turn negotiations, with fitness scores assigned via a reward function <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(S)</annotation></semantics></math>. The population then evolves through selection, crossover, and mutation operations, producing improved emotional policies <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{\omega}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{\omega}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for the next generation. These new policies are again evaluated through negotiation simulations to identify optimal policies <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\omega}^{i}*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ω</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\omega}^{i}*</annotation></semantics></math>, and the buyers’ emotion policy is updated accordingly. The optimization continues iteratively until the reward function converges.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Emotion-Aware MDPs</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize the negotiation process as a MDP, denoted <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{P},\mathcal{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{P},\mathcal{R})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The state space <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the negotiation context through tuples <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t,e_{t},p_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t,e_{t},p_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is the current number of turn of negotiation, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the agent’s emotion state drawn from a 7-dimensional emotion space <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> comprising <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">anger</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">disgust</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fear</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">happiness</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sadness</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">surprise</span>, and <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">neutral</span>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> encodes the price context, including both the current offer and historical price points.
The action space <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> encompasses all possible negotiation responses generated by the LLM.
Transition dynamics <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> are determined by the policy <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>, which governs emotional state transitions through <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t+1}\sim\pi_{\omega}(e|s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t+1}\sim\pi_{\omega}(e|s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\omega}(e|s_{t})=\text{softmax}(\text{LLM}(s_{t},e_{t},\tau_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>LLM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\omega}(e|s_{t})=\text{softmax}(\text{LLM}(s_{t},e_{t},\tau_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math> represents the emotion distribution generated by the LLM conditioned on the current state and temperature <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Policy Representation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each negotiation policy <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\omega}=(E,T,\mathbf{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐏</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\omega}=(E,T,\mathbf{P})</annotation></semantics></math> encodes three core components that govern the agent’s emotional behavior:
(1) an emotion sequence <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=(e_{1},...,e_{n})\in\mathcal{E}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=(e_{1},...,e_{n})\in\mathcal{E}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> defining the agent’s planned emotional trajectory across negotiation turns, where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the 7-dimensional emotion space;
(2) temperature parameters <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=(\tau_{0},\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=(\tau_{0},\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> that control response stochasticity through an exponential decay schedule <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(t)=\max(0.1,\tau_{0}\cdot(1-\delta)^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(t)=\max(0.1,\tau_{0}\cdot(1-\delta)^{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> determining initial exploration and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> governing the decay rate; and
(3) a transition matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}\in\mathbb{R}^{7\times 7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}\in\mathbb{R}^{7\times 7}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each entry <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{ij}=\mathbb{P}(e_{t+1}=j|e_{t}=i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{ij}=\mathbb{P}(e_{t+1}=j|e_{t}=i)</annotation></semantics></math> specifies the probability of transition between emotion states, constrained by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j=1}^{7}P_{ij}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>7</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j=1}^{7}P_{ij}=1</annotation></semantics></math> to maintain valid probability distributions across all emotional state transitions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Reward Function</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The evolutionary optimization evaluates policies through a reward function, which can be interpreted as a <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fitness</span> score (a term commonly used in evolutionary computing) measuring negotiation effectiveness:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(S)=\mathbf{1}_{\text{success}}\cdot\alpha\cdot\frac{b(S)}{e(S)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mtext>success</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(S)=\mathbf{1}_{\text{success}}\cdot\alpha\cdot\frac{b(S)}{e(S)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}_{\text{success}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mtext>success</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}_{\text{success}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an indicator function equal to 1 if negotiation succeeds and 0 otherwise; <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(S)\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(S)\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> represents the normalized buyer savings (as a percentage of the initial price); <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(S)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes negotiation efficiency, measured by the number of negotiation rounds; and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a weighting coefficient that balances the contribution of buyer savings relative to efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Evolved Reinforcement Learning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework formulates the search for an optimal emotional policy <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\omega}^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\omega}^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> as an evolutionary reinforcement learning problem, optimizing the policy’s emotional transition parameters through a generational cycle of evaluation and population-based refinement. Each iteration begins by deploying the current population of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> candidate policies <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\omega}^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\omega}^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> within the multi-turn negotiation simulation, which is governed by the LLM <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and a set of interaction prompts. The execution of each policy <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pi_{\omega}^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi_{\omega}^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> produces a full emotional state and dialogue sequence, whose performance is quantified by the reward function <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(S^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(S^{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Following this evaluation phase, policies are probabilistically selected for reproduction proportionally to their scaled reward, with selection probability</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\pi_{\omega}^{i})=\frac{\exp(R(S^{i})/\lambda)}{\sum_{j}\exp(R(S^{j})/\lambda)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\pi_{\omega}^{i})=\frac{\exp(R(S^{i})/\lambda)}{\sum_{j}\exp(R(S^{j})/\lambda)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> moderates selection pressure. To ensure elitism and monotonic improvement, the top <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> policies are preserved unchanged. Subsequently, genetic operators are applied: uniform crossover recombines parameters from selected parent policies with probability <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate offspring, while mutation randomly perturbs emotional parameters with probability <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> to sustain exploration.This process produces a new population of policies <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{\pi}{\omega}^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{\pi}{\omega}^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then an optimal emotional negotiation policy <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi{\omega}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi{\omega}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is selected which is then deployed in the next round of negotiation simulation to evaluate its performance and compute new rewards.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This algorithm terminates under one of two condition:
(1) the maximum fitness improvement remains below <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=0.01</annotation></semantics></math> for <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">five</span> consecutive generations, indicating convergence achieved; or
(2) the evolution process reaches <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> generations, exhausting the maximum computational budget.
This termination strategy balances thorough exploration of the policy space with computational efficiency, enabling the discovery of effective emotion transition patterns that preserve natural negotiation behavior.
The complete algorithmic implementation is presented in <a href="#S8.SS2" title="8.2 EvoEmo Algorithm ‣ 8 Appendix ‣ EvoEmo: Towards Evolved Emotional Policies for LLM Agents in Multi-Turn Negotiation" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 8.2</span></a> in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Setting</h2>

<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets and LLMs.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Likewise to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang and Hadfi (2024)</a></cite>, we selected a subset of negotiation cases from the CraigslistBargain dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2018</a>)</cite>—a widely-used benchmark for negotiation studies—for evaluation.
This subset comprises 20 distinct multi-turn negotiation scenarios spanning diverse product categories, including electronics, furniture, vehicles, and housing.
Each scenario specifies:
(1) product details (name, category, and description),
(2) agent-specific target prices for the seller, and
(3) emotion annotations that support realistic bargaining dynamics.
The selected dataset spans a wide price range ($50–$5,000) and includes varied item conditions (brand new or used), thereby enabling comprehensive evaluation of negotiation policies under heterogeneous market conditions.
Representative state-of-the-art LLMs from leading AI providers—GPT-5-mini (OpenAI), Gemini-2.5-Pro (Google), and DeepSeek-V3.1.1 (DeepSeek)—were selected to power both buyer and seller agents in our experiments.
These models were chosen for their demonstrated strengths in conversational capabilities, strategic reasoning performance, and accessibility via APIS, providing comprehensive coverage across diverse model architectures.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For evaluation, we focus on buyer agents with different emotion policies while keeping seller agents fixed in a <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vanilla</span> setting, where agents receive no explicit emotional prompts.
We define two baselines for comparison.
The first baseline comprises only <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">vanilla</span> agents, where neither buyer nor seller receives emotional guidance.
This setup ensures that both agents act solely according to their intrinsic emotional tendencies and strategic reasoning abilities, providing a reference point that reflects default negotiation behavior.
The second baseline pairs a vanilla seller and a <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">fixed-emotion</span> buyer, where the buyer maintains a constant emotional profile (e.g., happy or neutral) throughout the negotiation.
By comparing these baselines with a setup in which the buyer’s emotion is optimized via our proposed approach—<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EvoEmo</span>, we can quantify the impact of emotions on negotiation outcomes and assess the effectiveness of EvoEmo in enhancing LLM-agent-based, emotion-driven negotiations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Experimental Setup.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Simulated negotiations are conducted to evaluate the impact of different buyer emotion configurations—vanilla, fixed-emotion, or EvoEmo-optimized—on negotiation outcomes.
In all simulations, as mentioned earlier, sellers receive no emotional prompts, establishing a controlled environment to examine how the buyer’s emotional profile influences negotiation dynamics when interacting with a vanilla seller powered by LLMs trained on real-world data.
Moreover, all three LLMs described previously are employed to power both buyer and seller agents, resulting in <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">nine</span> distinct buyer-seller pairings for the negotiation experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All negotiations begin with the seller’s initial offer, as depicted in <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 EvoEmo Framework ‣ EvoEmo: Towards Evolved Emotional Policies for LLM Agents in Multi-Turn Negotiation" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 1</span></a>.
Performance is evaluated using three key metrics:
(1) <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Negotiation Success Rate</span> (%), the percentage of dialogues that result in an agreement;
(2) <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Buyer’s Savings</span> (%), the percentage of the reduction between the seller’s initial price and the final agreed-upon price; and
(3) <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Negotiation Efficiency</span>, the total number of dialogue turns between buyer and seller.
Experiments were conducted across all nine LLM pairings, and results compared the two baselines with EvoEmo, reported as the mean and standard deviation over 20 distinct negotiation scenarios.
The negotiation framework was implemented using LangGraph, with a maximum of 30 dialogue turns per negotiation session.
Additionally, a third-party agent based on <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4.1</span> was employed to serve as a mediator, monitoring the negotiation in real time.
This agent analyzes the dialogue history to classify each negotiation into one of three states:
(1) accepted (agreement reached),
(2) breakdown (negotiation failed), or
(3) ongoing (active negotiation).
This mechanism ensures consistent and impartial evaluation of negotiation outcomes across all simulations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Configuration and Ablation Studies</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">EvoEmo incorporates two key classes of configurable elements:
(1) evolutionary parameters, which define the core mechanics of the framework, and
(2) hyperparameters, which are tuned to achieve optimal performance.
The primary evolutionary parameters include the emotion transition matrix <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and the temperature decay <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, both optimized during evolution.
Four hyperparameters are systematically tuned:
elitism rate (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in{0.1,0.25,0.5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in{0.1,0.25,0.5}</annotation></semantics></math>),
mutation rate (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{m}\in{0.1,0.25,0.5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{m}\in{0.1,0.25,0.5}</annotation></semantics></math>),
crossover rate (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{c}\in{0.5,0.75,1.0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0.75</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{c}\in{0.5,0.75,1.0}</annotation></semantics></math>), and
population size (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in{10,20,50}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in{10,20,50}</annotation></semantics></math>).
The best-performing configurations from these search spaces are adopted to report experimental results.
Across all experiments, the number of evolutionary iterations is set to 5, and the temperature controlling emotion transition dynamics is fixed at 0.9.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the contribution of key design choices, we conduct ablation studies with the seller agent fixed to <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5-mini</span> and buyers varied across the three LLM architectures.
These studies evaluate:
(1) the effectiveness of the ratio-based reward function (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(S)/e(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(S)/e(S)</annotation></semantics></math>) compared to weighted-sum alternatives (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}b(S)+\alpha_{2}e(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}b(S)+\alpha_{2}e(S)</annotation></semantics></math>);
(2) robustness to variations in the emotion transition temperature; and
(3) stability across evolutionary iterations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.04310v1/figs/savings.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/366;" width="476" height="366" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Negotiation results in terms of buyer savings (%, <math id="S4.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>) across the nine buyer-seller pairs.
Black vertical lines on top of each bar indicate the 95% confidence interval (CI) for each setting.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experimental Results</h2>

<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.04310v1/success_rate_vs_efficiency.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/455;" width="476" height="455" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Negotiation results in terms of success rate (%, <math id="S5.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>) and negotiation efficiency (<math id="S5.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>, number of rounds of dialogue) across the nine buyer-seller pairings.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Buyers Savings</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Configuration and Ablation Studies ‣ 4 Experimental Setting ‣ EvoEmo: Towards Evolved Emotional Policies for LLM Agents in Multi-Turn Negotiation" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 2</span></a>, the proposed framework—EvoEmo—consistently achieves the <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">highest</span> buyer savings across all buyer-seller pairings, demonstrating its clear superiority over both baselines (vanilla and fixed-emotion settings).
The results reveal two intriguing insights:
<span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">First</span>, with respect to emotional strategies, buyers adopting fixed <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">negative</span> emotions (e.g., anger and sad) frequently outperform the vanilla baseline.
This effect is particularly evident for multiple seller models facing the buyers who continuously express disgust or sadness, suggesting that LLM seller agents are more likely to concede when confronted with persistently negative emotional signals. This finding underscores that sustained negative affects are non-trivial factors that significantly influence negotiation dynamics and outcomes.
Conversely, buyers with fixed positive emotions, such as happy and surprise, obtain lower savings relative to the vanilla, indicating that seller agents defend prices more effectively when interpreting the buyer’s mood as positive, perhaps perceiving less urgency to offer concessions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Second</span>, performance differs markedly across LLMs.
On the seller side, Gemini-2.5-pro demonstrates the strongest price defense against both vanilla and fixed-emotion buyers, yet remains vulnerable to the adaptive emotion optimized by EvoEmo.
On the buyer side, the results are model-dependent: the Gemini-based buyer achieves the highest savings against the GPT-5-mini seller, while the GPT-5-mini buyer performs most effectively against the DeepSeek-V3.1 seller.
Notably, no buyer model demonstrated a significant advantage when negotiating against the robust Gemini-2.5-pro seller, highlighting its strength as a challenging negotiation opponent.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Success vs. Efficiency</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Buyers equipped with the EvoEmo-optimized emotion profile consistently achieve near 100% success rates and do so more efficiently than those using vanilla or fixed-emotion settings, requiring way fewer rounds to reach an agreement.
These results clearly demonstrate EvoEmo’s superiority over both baselines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Interestingly, negotiation efficiency shows no strong correlation with any specific fixed emotional strategy, including the vanilla baseline.
However, a clear pattern emerges in success rates: buyers employing fixed <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">positive</span> emotions, as well as the vanilla setting without predefined emotions, consistently achieve higher success rates than those using fixed <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">negative</span> emotions.
This suggests that although negative emotions may occasionally lead to more favorable agreed prices, they inherently carry a higher risk of negotiation breakdown.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ablation Study</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ EvoEmo: Towards Evolved Emotional Policies for LLM Agents in Multi-Turn Negotiation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the ablation study results on the importance of the reward function.
The ratio-based function (<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{b(S)}{e(S)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{b(S)}{e(S)}</annotation></semantics></math>) outperforms the weighted alternative (<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(S)-e(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(S)-e(S)</annotation></semantics></math>), achieving very close savings while requiring substantially fewer negotiation rounds (34.6% faster to reach agreement).
This demonstrates its superior ability to balance financial gains with negotiation efficiency.
Crucially, the performance gap between reward functions highlights that emotion transition dynamics significantly impact negotiation outcomes, validating emotion as a non-trivial factor in negotiation strategies.
Further ablation study results are provided in <a href="#S8.SS1" title="8.1 More Experiments ‣ 8 Appendix ‣ EvoEmo: Towards Evolved Emotional Policies for LLM Agents in Multi-Turn Negotiation" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 8.1</span></a> in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Negotiation performance comparison between different reward function formulations.</span></figcaption>
