<td id="S5.T1.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T1.5.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">39.4</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.5.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T1.5.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">100.0</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.5.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T1.5.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">5.8</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.5.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Improv.</span></th>
<td id="S5.T1.5.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.5.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">-2.7%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.5.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.5.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">-</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.5.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.5.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">+22.7%</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While this study shows that emotion is a <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non trivial</span> factor in a negotiation—and a buyer agent with adaptive emotion profile, optimized via EvoEmo, can achieve much better performance in price negotiation scenarios—it also has several limitations that need further research:</p>
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limited Emotional Spectrum and Baseline Comparisons.</span>
This study considers only seven basic emotional states, which may not capture the full complexity of human emotional expression in real negotiations.
Additionally, the baseline comparisons are limited to fixed-emotion and emotion-neutral strategies, omitting potentially informative comparisons such as random emotional sequences.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scenario Dependence and Generalization Challenges.</span>
The evaluation is conducted on 20 daily negotiation scenarios focusing on conventional commercial contexts, raising questions about potential selection bias and limited generalizability.
EvoEmo’s effectiveness remains unverified in more diverse negotiation domains, particularly high-stakes, emotionally charged scenarios such as debt collection, legal dispute resolution, or crisis negotiations where emotional dynamics may differ substantially from standard commercial settings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interpretability of Emotional Strategies.</span>
The black-box nature of LLM responses and evolutionary optimization makes it difficult to explain why specific emotional sequences succeed in particular negotiation contexts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulation-to-Reality Gap.</span>
Validation through LLM simulations may not capture human expertise, and computational intensity limits real-time adaptation in practical deployments.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper examines the role of emotion in LLM-based negotiation and introduces EvoEmo, an evolutionary reinforcement learning framework for optimizing emotional sequences.
Our results show that emotion is a non-trivial factor in successful negotiation, with EvoEmo’s optimized emotional policies consistently improving performance by achieving higher success rates, greater efficiency, and enhanced buyer savings compared to both vanilla and fixed-emotion baselines.
The ability to dynamically adapt emotional states proves critical for effective multi-turn bargaining, enabling agents to strategically leverage emotional intelligence in negotiation.
Beyond the limitations outlined in <a href="#S6" title="6 Limitations ‣ EvoEmo: Towards Evolved Emotional Policies for LLM Agents in Multi-Turn Negotiation" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 6</span></a>, future work will examine the ethical implications and behavioral consistency of evolved policies, with particular attention to the emergence of deceptive or compromising behaviors.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
